{"id":"e0a2d885e8114ec1d02e59ea38de632edf47f1189fb26f532cd55c2ab9eab573","lamport":1,"author":"scual.project@gmail.com","created_at":"2026-10-10T12:12:27.821657945Z","format_version":1,"kind":"set-field","payload":{"fields":{"description":"## What to build\n\nImporting a new ticket from a fork, and merging a ticket that exists on both sides, become a single lib operation that produces Operations (and set/comment changes), with the guard policy for closing in one place. `ti pull` shrinks to fetch plus report.\n\n## Why\n\nBy code reading (not reproduced): the importer writes title, status, state, assignee and other scalars as plain keys with no format version and no ops, which ADR 0002 retired. The merge path writes `parent` as a plain key, which is silently ignored for any ticket that already has ops. Fork-merge appears to work for new tickets only because the legacy read path covers it. The forced-lifecycle bypass used for sync is also called from two places in the binary.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] First reproduce: a failing test where a fork changes the parent of an op-based ticket and the merge loses it\n- [ ] Import and merge of a ticket emit Operations; no ticket scalars are written as plain keys\n- [ ] A lib test with two temporary repos imports a ticket, merges a parent difference, and `verify` passes\n- [ ] Forced-lifecycle and closed-by bypasses are used from one place only\n- [ ] The existing cross-repo `ti pull` CLI test passes unchanged\n\n## Blocked by\n\nNone — can start immediately.\n\n## Notes\n\nFixes a violation of ADR 0002. Suggested to do first, together with the history ticket."}}}